<h1>Mike Quigley</h1>
<p>US Representative, 5th District. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>In Congress since 2009 and a Cook County commissioner for a decade before that; 68, has lived in Lakeview since 1982, married with two daughters. Filed on the first day and calls this his last and biggest fight.</p>
<p><a href="/directdemocracy/app/candidate/4oD2IFEWjzeL4Q7iu3zq8EefPqY2">Full platform, open to comments</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.quigleyforchicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (quigleyforchicago.com)</a></p>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Friends of Mike Quigley</li>
<li>Raised: $332K</li>
<li>Spent: $544K</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $343K</li>
<li>Raised July 1 to Sept. 30, 2026; spent Jan. 1 to Sept. 30, 2026. Includes a $200,000 transfer from his congressional committee. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=40800" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>3.6% of likely voters in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 6.6 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>What they would do</h2>
<ul>
<li>Says the next mayor has to be willing to lose the job to fix the city&#39;s finances. <q>The next mayor has to be willing to risk the job to save the city.</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/fran-spielman-show/2026/08/13/rep-mike-quigley-mayoral-candidate-chicago" rel="nofollow noopener">Sun-Times, Aug. 13, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Build or preserve 50,000 homes in four years and legalize four-flats and backyard units citywide. <q>Our goal is to provide affordable housing, which is critical for safety and generational wealth</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/fran-spielman-show/2026/08/13/rep-mike-quigley-mayoral-candidate-chicago" rel="nofollow noopener">Sun-Times, Aug. 13, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Move 600 officers from desk jobs back to patrol instead of hiring 1,000 more, and restructure how city government operates. <q>We are not going to be able to provide basic city services unless we restructure how our government operates,</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/mike-quigley-chicago-mayoral-campaign-kickoff/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, June 27, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Reopen neighborhood mental health clinics and send mental health teams to crisis calls.</li>
</ul>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>Main points</h3>
<p>Housing: Build or preserve 50,000 homes in four years, allow four-flats and backyard units across the city, and send TIF money to affordable housing. No new rent restrictions.</p>
<p>Police: Move 600 officers from desk jobs back to patrol, cap overtime, and replace eight oversight bodies with one elected board.</p>
<p>Budget and pensions: Start with the savings a city-paid report already identified, make raising taxes the last option, and make honest trade-offs on pensions so they do not fail.</p>
<h3>Next to the others</h3>
<p>Housing target: Quigley and Giannoulias both aim for 50,000 homes.</p>
<p>Police staffing: Quigley would redeploy desk officers rather than hire; Kelly and Mendoza would hire or fill vacancies; Wilson wants four superintendents.</p>
<p>Pensions: Quigley talks about changing what people pay in and when they collect; Kitover would freeze taxes until Springfield reforms pensions; Giannoulias would consolidate the funds.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Would start with the hundreds of millions in yearly savings a city-paid report already identified, treat raising taxes as the last option, and tell people plainly that the pension funds will fail without trade-offs.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>The last option needs to be raising taxes.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/mike-quigley-chicago-mayoral-campaign-kickoff/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, June 27, 2026</a>)</li>
<li><q>We&#39;re not gonna take away your pension, but it will fail,</q> (<a href="https://chicago.suntimes.com/fran-spielman-show/2026/08/13/rep-mike-quigley-mayoral-candidate-chicago" rel="nofollow noopener">Sun-Times, Aug. 13, 2026</a>)</li>
<li><q>Every dollar that we are wasting is a dollar we can&#39;t spend on housing, public safety, parks, mental health, our kids.</q> (<a href="https://www.quigleyforchicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">quigleyforchicago.com</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>Correct. Tavern-style. His pick is Pat&#39;s Pizzeria on Lincoln Avenue, and he delivered for Leona&#39;s and served slices at Al &amp; Joe&#39;s on his Undercover Congressman shifts.</p>
<p><q>My visit to Pat&#39;s made it clear that there&#39;s nothing quite like Chicago&#39;s tavern-style pizza</q> (<a href="https://quigley.house.gov/media-center/press-releases/quigley-defends-chicago-pizza-superiority" rel="nofollow noopener">Press release, May 31, 2024</a>)</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>Has taken 200 stitches playing hockey, which he has played since he was six, and co-chairs the Congressional Hockey Caucus. (<a href="https://www.quigleyforchicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">quigleyforchicago.com</a>)</li>
<li>Worked a garbage truck, a hot dog stand, and the Dinkel&#39;s bakery counter as the Undercover Congressman. (<a href="https://quigley.house.gov/about/undercover-congressman" rel="nofollow noopener">quigley.house.gov</a>)</li>
</ul>
